---
title: Customize & Regenerate Theme
---

Now that you know how `theme.json` is structured, it's time to edit it in your local clone and regenerate the Compose theme resources the app actually reads.

## Edit a Token

In your local clone of `vonage-video-android-app`, open `theme.json` and change one or more tokens under `colors`, `borderRadius`, or `typography.mobile`. For example, update the primary brand color for both light and dark mode, and increase the medium border radius:

```json
{
  "colors": {
    "light": { "primary": "#1D6FE6", "on-primary": "#FFFFFF" },
    "dark":  { "primary": "#5FA0FF", "on-primary": "#000000" }
  },
  "borderRadius": {
    "medium": 10
  }
}
```

Edit these values in place, keep every existing color role, and save the file.

{% aside %}
Only `typography.mobile` affects Android; `font-family` is ignored.
{% /aside %}

## Regenerate the Theme Resources

From the root of your local clone, manually run the theme generator Gradle task:

```sh
./gradlew generateTheme
```

The `ThemeGeneratorPlugin` reads `theme.json` and regenerates `Color.kt`, `Shape.kt`, `Typography.kt`, and `Theme.kt` in:

```
vonage-video-ui-compose/src/main/java/com/vonage/android/compose/theme/
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
Do **not** edit those four generated files directly — the theme task overwrites them. Always make your changes in `theme.json` instead.
{% /aside %}

## Rebuild the App

```sh
./gradlew installDebug
```

Or in Android Studio, press the ▶️ **Run** button (or **Shift+F10**) with your emulator or device selected.

Next, you'll confirm your changes in the running app.
